      "model": "claude-fable-5-1",
      "question": "Something may have spiked in Transformer 1's oil at MAIN recently, and I'd like it checked properly rather than by eye. Screen acetylene, co and hydrogen between 2024-01-15T00:00:00.000000 and 2024-01-19T00:00:00.000000 for anomalous samples. For each channel, list any flagged timestamps with their values, and give the total number of anomalies per channel. Then tell me whether flagged points on different gases fall on the same timestamp. If acetylene and hydrogen are both flagged at the same timestamp, treat it as possible arcing and recommend an immediate confirmatory resample. If only co is flagged, treat it as possible paper involvement and recommend a routine retest. If nothing is flagged, say the window looks stable. Use a proper anomaly detection model, not a fixed threshold. If the detector can't run because a channel has fewer than 4 non-null samples, report that channel's min, max and mean instead, and flag any sample more than 50% above that channel's mean as a manual outlier.",
      "answer": "Nothing further to request \u2014 every route to a file pointer is exhausted (no catalog detector cards, no `materialize_iot` tool exposed, no pre-materialized file in the server workspace, and `iot://` isn't a supported protocol). Here is the result.\n\n## Transformer 1 (MAIN) \u2014 DGA spike screen, 2024\u201101\u201115 \u2192 2024\u201101\u201119\n\n**Detector status: could not run.** All three channels have 4 non\u2011null samples (daily, 01\u201115 to 01\u201118), so the \u22654\u2011sample rule was met \u2014 but the TSFM anomaly path requires a file pointer to the series, and this session has no tool that can materialize IoT data to a file (`materialize_iot` is not available, no catalog detectors exist, and probed workspace paths don't exist). I've applied your fallback screen instead, and I'm being explicit that this is a manual statistic, not a model.\n\n### Per\u2011channel fallback (min / max / mean; manual\u2011outlier rule = >50 % above mean)\n\n| Channel | Samples (01\u201115, 16, 17, 18) | Min | Max | Mean | Outlier threshold | Flagged | Count |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| acetylene (ppm) | 7, 6, 8, 7 | 6 | 8 | 7.0 | > 10.5 | none | 0 |\n| co (ppm) | 32, 30, 35, 33 | 30 | 35 | 32.5 | > 48.75 | none | 0 |\n| hydrogen (ppm) | 2845, 2950, 2800, 2900 | 2800 | 2950 | 2873.75 | > 4310.6 | none | 0 |\n\n- **Flagged timestamps:** none on any channel.\n- **Cross\u2011gas coincidence:** not applicable \u2014 no flags on any channel, so no shared timestamps.\n\n### Verdict\n**The window looks stable.** No spike is evident in acetylene, CO, or hydrogen; each channel varies within \u00b15 % of its mean across the four samples. No arcing (C\u2082H\u2082 + H\u2082) or paper\u2011involvement (CO\u2011only) pattern is triggered.\n\n### Two caveats\n1. **This is not the model\u2011based screen you asked for.** If you can expose the TSFM `materialize_iot` tool (or drop a CSV with `timestamp, acetylene, co, hydrogen` into `/tmp/tsfm_work/`), I can rerun immediately with the `SubLOF` detector recipe and give you a proper result.\n2. **Stability \u2260 health.** Hydrogen is sitting at ~2,800\u20132,950 ppm on every sample, which is far above typical IEC 60599 in\u2011service limits even though it isn't *spiking*. That's worth a Rogers\u2011ratio interpretation of a full gas set (CH\u2084, C\u2082H\u2084, C\u2082H\u2086 included) \u2014 say the word and I'll run it.",
