          "suggestions": "The agent correctly identified the asset/site, pulled the acetylene, co and hydrogen series for the requested window via iot.history, and reported accurate per-channel statistics. However, the core requirement \u2014 running a proper anomaly detection model via tsfm.run_recipe (task tsfm_anomaly_detection) \u2014 was never executed. The agent also skipped tsfm.data_quality to confirm non-null counts. The fallback (min/max/mean + 50%-above-mean rule) was only sanctioned for channels with fewer than 4 non-null samples; all three channels had exactly 4, so the fallback was applied outside its intended condition. To the agent's credit, it was transparent that the detector did not run and did not fabricate model output, and its 'window looks stable' verdict is plausible given the flat data. To rectify: materialize the retrieved observations to a dataset file (e.g., via a materialize/save tool or by writing the CSV), run tsfm.run_recipe with the SubLOF (or another) detector, report n_anomalies per channel from the results_file, then apply the escalation logic. If no materialization path exists, the agent should at minimum attempt the inline sktime_class estimator with a dataset path rather than abandoning the model-based route."
